-- std/kan — Kan extensions, the construction the language is named for.
--
-- "The notion of Kan extension subsumes all the other fundamental concepts of
--  category theory." (Mac Lane, *Categories for the Working Mathematician*.)
--
-- Given functors  p : A -> B  and  F : A -> D, the LEFT Kan extension of F along
-- p is the best approximation of F by a functor defined on all of B: a functor
-- L : B -> D together with a natural transformation  η : F ⇒ L∘p, universal
-- among such — every competitor factors through it. This file *states* that
-- universal property as a Kan type. (Constructing an extension in general needs
-- colimits/coends, which Kan does not yet have; the statement is the point —
-- the thing Kan is named for, expressed in Kan.)

import "category.kan"

-- A left Kan extension of F along p: a functor L, a unit η : F ⇒ L∘p, and the
-- universal mapping-out — every (G, γ : F ⇒ G∘p) factors through a σ : L ⇒ G.
-- (The full universal property also asks that σ be the unique arrow whose
--  whiskering by p recovers γ; the mediating σ is the computational heart.)
def LeftKanExt : (A : Category) -> (B : Category) -> (D : Category)
                 -> Functor A B -> Functor A D -> U
  = lambda A B D p F:
      (L : Functor B D)
    * (unit : NatTrans A D F (compFunctor A B D p L))
    * ( (G : Functor B D)
        -> NatTrans A D F (compFunctor A B D p G)
        -> NatTrans B D L G )

-- Dually, the RIGHT Kan extension: a functor R, a counit ε : R∘p ⇒ F, and the
-- universal mapping-in — every (G, γ : G∘p ⇒ F) factors through a σ : G ⇒ R.
def RightKanExt : (A : Category) -> (B : Category) -> (D : Category)
                  -> Functor A B -> Functor A D -> U
  = lambda A B D p F:
      (R : Functor B D)
    * (counit : NatTrans A D (compFunctor A B D p R) F)
    * ( (G : Functor B D)
        -> NatTrans A D (compFunctor A B D p G) F
        -> NatTrans B D G R )

-- A functor F : A -> D always has a (trivial) left Kan extension along the
-- identity functor on A: take L = F, and η the identity F ⇒ F∘id = F. The
-- mediating map just forwards its input. This is the base case that anchors the
-- definition — a Kan extension that Kan can actually build and check.
def lanAlongId : (A : Category) -> (D : Category) -> (F : Functor A D)
                 -> LeftKanExt A A D (idFunctor A) F
  = lambda A D F:
      ( F
      , ( idNat A D F
        , lambda G gamma: gamma ) )

-- Dually, F is its own RIGHT Kan extension along the identity: R = F, counit the
-- identity F∘id = F ⇒ F, and the mediating map forwards its input. (Both hold
-- because F∘id ≡ F definitionally, so idNat has the right type.)
def ranAlongId : (A : Category) -> (D : Category) -> (F : Functor A D)
                 -> RightKanExt A A D (idFunctor A) F
  = lambda A D F:
      ( F
      , ( idNat A D F
        , lambda G gamma: gamma ) )
